// Where a tick's claims become the tick's plan. Two steps, in this order: the
// placeholders a claimless branch sends are dropped, then what is left is
// bounded by AR_DISPATCH_BATCH_CAP. The whole module behind the marker above is
// spliced in, so clampIntervalSeconds arrives beside capBatch and goes unused
// here, the reschedule it states being SQL already, inside the claims.
//
// The drop is keyed on the ABSENT unit_kind rather than on which branch an item
// came in on, because the Merge node in front is where a branch stops being
// readable: past it an item is an item, and the only handle left is what a
// claim put IN the row. A Postgres node whose statement is not a SELECT emits
// one success item when it returns no rows, so a tick with nothing due anywhere
// arrives here as two items, and a half-empty one as real rows plus a
// placeholder. No placeholder carries a unit_kind and every claimed row does.
//
// Dropping before capping is why these are two steps rather than one filtered
// slice. A placeholder counted toward the cap spends a slot on a row that does
// not exist, so a tick with one branch idle would dispatch one fewer than the
// operator set, and nothing about a short pass says why.
//
// This is the SECOND place the cap is applied and it is not a re-check of an
// already-bounded batch. Each claim carries the same setting as a SQL LIMIT, so
// a LIMIT bounds a BRANCH and this call bounds the TICK: a pass finding both
// tables backlogged claims twice the cap and dispatches the cap. What it drops
// was already CLAIMED, the claim having moved next_run_at before anything
// looked at the row, so a dropped row waits a whole interval rather than being
// taken by the tick after this one. capBatch in src/lib/schedule.ts carries the
// rest, including why a LIMIT one edit away from being tuned off is not the
// copy to rely on.
//
// The setting is quoted and parsed rather than spliced in bare. A marker
// resolves to whatever the settings chain answered, and quoting is what keeps
// every possible answer a valid literal: an unparseable one becomes NaN and
// comes back as capBatch's own refusal naming the value, where a bare marker
// would leave a ReferenceError naming nothing.
//
// What arrives here is NOT ordered by how overdue a row is. A claim's ORDER BY
// picks WHICH rows it takes and never the order they come back in, and the
// merge concatenates one branch after the other whatever either returned. So
// the cap takes the front of an unordered stream: what it drops is arbitrary
// among the rows claimed, not the least overdue among them.
//
// Arbitrary is not the same as random: the merge puts input 0 first, so every
// topics claim precedes every export claim. A topics table backlogged past the
// cap on its own therefore leaves no slot for an export subscription at all,
// and those rows wait a whole interval on every tick it stays that way.
const CAP = Number('__ENVVAR:AR_DISPATCH_BATCH_CAP__');
const claimed = $input.all().map((i) => i.json).filter((r) => r && r.unit_kind != null);
return capBatch(claimed, CAP).map((unit) => ({ json: unit }));
